A JWT (JSON Web Token) is a string that contains encoded JSON data, which is digitally signed so it can’t be tampered with.
Authentication & Authorization Purpose JWT Token wil use


                                                                                        
                                                                                           JWT Format (Structure)


A JWT has 3 parts separated by dots (.): HEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE
Example: eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJ1c2VyIjoiYWRtaW4iLCJyb2xlIjoiYWRtaW4ifQ.SflKxwRJSMeKKF2QT4fwpMeJf36POk6yJV_adQssw5c



1️⃣ Header

Contains:

Algorithm (e.g., HS256)
Token type (JWT)
{
  "alg": "HS256",
  "typ": "JWT"
}

2️⃣ Payload (Important 🔥)

Contains actual data (claims):

{
  "user": "admin",
  "role": "admin"
}


3️⃣ Signature

Used to verify integrity:

HMACSHA256(
  base64UrlEncode(header) + "." + base64UrlEncode(payload),
  secret
)

👉 If payload is modified → signature becomes invalid




RS256 > HS256 if accpected valid vuln {JWT Token algorithm confusion }

                                                                                        JWT Signature Bypass

Capture JWT token from request 
Go to https://www.jwt.io/
Paste the JWT token
Modify payload (example: "role":"user" → "role":"admin")
Copy the modified token
Paste it back into the request
Forward the request
If response is 200 OK and admin access is granted → Vulnerble



Capture JWT token from request
Go to https://www.jwt.io/
Decode the token (Header, Payload, Signature)
Change header:
"alg": "HS256" → "alg": "none"
Modify payload:
"role": "user" → "role": "admin"
Remove the signature part completely
Final token format:
HEADER.PAYLOAD.
Paste modified token in request
Forward the request
If response is 200 OK and admin access is granted → JWT alg=none vulnerability


 
                                                                                JWT Weak secret vulnerability

Capture JWT token from request (Authorization: Bearer )
Go to https://www.jwt.io/ and decode the token (Header, Payload, Signature)
Identify the algorithm (example: HS256 → uses a secret key)
Use a tool (like jwt_tool or hashcat) to brute-force the secret key
Once the correct secret key is found, modify the payload
(example: "role":"user" → "role":"admin")
Re-sign the token using the discovered secret key
Generate a new valid JWT (with correct signature)
Paste the modified token in the request
Forward the request
If response is 200 OK and admin access is granted


